bug_report:
[12 Regression] Incorrect "-Wmaybe-uninitialized" warning from GCC 13.2.0
The problem occurs with GCC 13.2.0, and the submitted code also produces the warning in GCC 12.2.0, but minor variations on the code tested while reducing the production code did not always reproduce with 12 even though it did with 13. I also tested versions 7.3.0, 8.2.0, 9.3.0, 10.3.0, 11.2.0; none of them produced the warning for me.  I'm not clear if this is a regression in 13 (and 12), or whether the older versions are oblivious to the and 12 and 13 are trying to spot a problem but not recognizing why there isn't a problem in the code.

The gist of the problem is that a variable is defined but not initialized immediately:

    uint16_t pg_version;
    …
    if (flags == PGR_VERSION)
        pg_version = GET_PGVERS(pg_old);
    …
    for (…)
    {
        …
        if (flags == PGR_VERSION)
        {
            cur_rowid = ROWID(PAGE(sav_rowid), i, PT_BIGRID(cur_partp));
            pg_expand(GET_SLOTPTR(pg_old, old_slot), row_data, pg_version);
        }
    }

GCC complains that pg_version may be used uninitialized, but pg_value is only used in the loop body if the condition that assigned it a value outside the loop applies inside the body too.

The workaround is simple: initialize pg_version to 0 when it is defined.


